The most important boundary is between the generation pipeline and the model providers. Code should request operations such as “classify this passage” or “generate these cards.” Provider adapters handle the actual API calls. That lets you replace JEV or the cheap generator without rewriting the application.

A few rules would keep this manageable:

One documented installation command. The root Compose configuration starts the application and its dependencies.
Version prompts with code. Save the prompt, model, and pipeline versions used for each generation job.
Keep evaluation separate from production. Experiments establish where JEV helps; users get the selected pipeline.
Keep uploaded documents, generated decks, credentials, and database files out of Git. Only small, redistributable test fixtures belong in the repository.
Keep licensing explicit. LICENSE should contain the terms you ultimately choose; don’t add an open-source license automatically.

For the first commit, I’d include the spec, README, architecture outline, environment example, and a working application skeleton. Add the remaining directories as their functionality is implemented—no need to populate an elaborate empty structure.

I’d also keep JEV out of the repository name. The product is document-to-flashcards; JEV is one implementation choice.

JevRecall would be my pick. It communicates studying and memory, works beyond medicine, and sounds like a product people could pay to use.

